(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-11"] = {"phy-12-11-m01":{"idea":"A suspended magnet lines up with the Earth's field, so it points north-south.","steps":["The Earth behaves like a big bar magnet.","Its field turns a freely hanging magnet into the magnetic meridian.","So the magnet points roughly along geographic north-south."],"others":"East-west is wrong. The Earth is a magnet, so 'any direction' is wrong. 'Vertical everywhere' is true only near the magnetic poles.","trap":"This is how a compass works.","answer":"approximately along the geographic north-south direction"},"phy-12-11-m02":{"idea":"A dipole in a field feels a torque of size mB sin θ that turns it towards the field.","steps":["In vector form τ = m × B.","Its size is mB sin θ.","It tends to align the dipole with the field."],"others":"m/(B sin θ) has the wrong dependence. cos θ would give zero torque at 90°. Turning away from the field is the reverse of what happens.","trap":"Zero torque at 0° and 180°.","answer":"τ = mB sin θ, and it tends to align the dipole with the field"},"phy-12-11-m03":{"idea":"A dipole has the lowest energy when it points along the field.","steps":["Take U = 0 at θ = 90°.","Work done by the field turning it gives U = −mB cos θ.","At θ = 0°, U = −mB is the lowest value."],"others":"A constant mB ignores the angle. −mB sin θ and +mB cos θ have the lowest value at the wrong angle.","trap":"Stable: θ = 0°. Unstable: θ = 180°.","answer":"U = −mB cos θ, which is the lowest when the dipole is along the field"},"phy-12-11-m04":{"idea":"A magnet slightly turned from the field swings back and forth, like a pendulum.","steps":["For a small angle the torque is −mBθ.","That is like a spring torque, so the motion is simple harmonic.","ω = √(mB/I), so T = 2π√(I/(mB))."],"others":"It does not stay put, because there is a restoring torque. T = 2π√(mB/I) has the ratio upside down. It does not move away, because the equilibrium is stable.","trap":"Stronger field, shorter period.","answer":"oscillate about the field direction, with a period T = 2π√(I/(mB))"},"phy-12-11-m05":{"idea":"A current loop is a magnetic dipole with moment current times area.","steps":["m = I A for one turn.","The direction comes from the right-hand rule.","For N turns it is N I A."],"others":"IA² and I²A have wrong powers. I/A has wrong units. The direction is not along the current.","trap":"Unit is A m².","answer":"m = IA, in the direction given by the right-hand rule"},"phy-12-11-m06":{"idea":"Magnetism in materials comes from the electrons.","steps":["An orbiting electron is a tiny current loop.","The electron also has a spin moment.","Nuclear moments are about 2000 times smaller."],"others":"Nuclei, neutrons and protons contribute very little compared with electrons.","trap":"Orbit and spin of electrons.","answer":"the orbital motion and the spin of the electrons in the atoms"},"phy-12-11-m07":{"idea":"An orbiting electron is a current loop, so its moment is proportional to its angular momentum.","steps":["Current I = e/T.","With L = mₑ v r and area π r², m = I A = (e/2mₑ)L.","The electron is negative, so m points opposite to L."],"others":"The same direction as L would be right for a positive charge. m = eL and (2mₑ/e)L do not follow.","trap":"Negative charge means opposite direction.","answer":"m = (e/2mₑ)L, and it is opposite in direction to L"},"phy-12-11-m08":{"idea":"The Bohr magneton is the moment of the first Bohr orbit.","steps":["In the first orbit, L = h/2π.","m = (e/2mₑ)(h/2π) = eh/(4πmₑ).","The value is about 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ A m²."],"others":"mₑh/(4πe) has the constants upside down. The same formula with 10⁻²⁷ has the wrong power. eh/(2mₑ) lacks the 2π.","trap":"Remember 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴.","answer":"eh/(4πmₑ), about 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ A m²"},"phy-12-11-m09":{"idea":"Magnetisation is magnetic moment per unit volume.","steps":["M = m_net/V.","Unit: A/m.","A strongly magnetised material has a large M."],"others":"The net moment of the whole material is not per volume. B in tesla is the field. Force per unit area is pressure.","trap":"M and H have the same unit, A/m.","answer":"the net magnetic moment per unit volume of the material"},"phy-12-11-m10":{"idea":"The field in a material has a part from currents (H) and a part from the material (M).","steps":["H comes from the external currents.","M comes from the magnetisation of the material.","Together B = μ₀(H + M)."],"others":"μ₀HM multiplies, not adds. (H + M)/μ₀ divides. μ₀(H − M) subtracts, which is wrong.","trap":"For a linear material, M = χH, so B = μ₀(1 + χ)H.","answer":"B = μ₀(H + M)"},"phy-12-11-m11":{"idea":"Susceptibility tells how easily a material is magnetised.","steps":["χ = M/H.","It has no unit.","The relative permeability is μᵣ = 1 + χ."],"others":"H/M is inverted. M × H is not a ratio. B/H is the permeability, not the susceptibility.","trap":"Susceptibility is M over H.","answer":"χ = M/H, and the relative permeability is 1 + χ"},"phy-12-11-m12":{"idea":"Diamagnetic materials are weakly repelled, so their susceptibility is small and negative.","steps":["The induced moment is opposite to the applied field.","That makes χ negative.","The size is about 10⁻⁵, which is small."],"others":"Large values are for ferromagnets. A positive sign is for paramagnets.","trap":"Dia: small negative. Para: small positive. Ferro: large positive.","answer":"small and negative"},"phy-12-11-m13":{"idea":"Paramagnets have a small positive susceptibility that falls with temperature (Curie's law).","steps":["The atoms have permanent moments, and the field lines them up.","Heat disturbs this order.","χ = C/T, so it decreases when T rises."],"others":"A rise with temperature is the opposite of Curie's law. A large susceptibility is for ferromagnets. A negative value is for diamagnets.","trap":"χ ∝ 1/T.","answer":"small and positive, and it decreases when the temperature rises"},"phy-12-11-m14":{"idea":"By Curie's law, susceptibility is inversely proportional to absolute temperature.","steps":["χ ∝ 1/T.","T doubles from 300 K to 600 K.","χ halves: 3.0 × 10⁻⁴/2 = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴."],"others":"3.0 × 10⁻⁴ ignores the change. 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ doubles it. 0.75 × 10⁻⁴ halves it twice.","trap":"Use kelvin.","answer":"1.5 × 10⁻⁴"},"phy-12-11-m15":{"idea":"Ferromagnets have domains, which give a very large positive susceptibility.","steps":["A domain is a small region in which atomic moments are all lined up.","In an applied field, domains along the field grow and others turn.","That gives a very large magnetisation."],"others":"Zero or small susceptibility does not suit iron, nickel and cobalt. A negative one is for diamagnets.","trap":"Domains are the key idea.","answer":"a very large positive susceptibility, because of domains"},"phy-12-11-m16":{"idea":"Heat above the Curie temperature destroys the domains.","steps":["At the Curie temperature the thermal energy beats the forces that align the moments.","The domains break up.","The material becomes paramagnetic."],"others":"It does not stay ferromagnetic. It does not become diamagnetic. It does not become a stronger magnet.","trap":"Iron's Curie temperature is about 770 °C.","answer":"becomes paramagnetic, since the thermal motion breaks up the domains"},"phy-12-11-m17":{"idea":"A diamagnetic rod is pushed away from strong fields and sets across the field.","steps":["A diamagnet is weakly repelled.","So it moves to where the field is weaker.","A long rod of it sets at right angles to the field."],"others":"Moving to the stronger field and setting along it is the paramagnetic behaviour. It does move, even though weakly.","trap":"Paramagnetic rod sets along the field.","answer":"moves towards the weaker part of the field, and sets across the field"},"phy-12-11-m18":{"idea":"Retentivity is the magnetism that stays after the field is removed.","steps":["Reduce H to zero on the B–H curve.","The B that is still there is the retentivity (remanence).","The coercivity is the reverse H needed to make B zero."],"others":"Susceptibility and saturation are different quantities. Coercivity is the reverse field, not the leftover B.","trap":"Retentivity: leftover B. Coercivity: reverse H.","answer":"the retentivity (remanence)"},"phy-12-11-m19":{"idea":"The area of the hysteresis loop is the energy lost as heat per cycle.","steps":["Each cycle of magnetisation needs work.","That work is proportional to the loop area.","It is lost as heat."],"others":"The area is not the maximum field or the retentivity. The energy is lost, not stored permanently.","trap":"Transformer cores need a thin loop.","answer":"the energy lost as heat in one cycle of magnetisation"},"phy-12-11-m20":{"idea":"Soft iron is chosen for cores because it magnetises and demagnetises easily.","steps":["It has a high permeability.","Its coercivity is low and its hysteresis loop is thin.","So little energy is lost each cycle."],"others":"Zero susceptibility would give no magnetisation. Low permeability and wide loops would waste energy. High retentivity and coercivity belong to permanent magnets.","trap":"Soft iron for cores. Steel for permanent magnets.","answer":"high permeability, low coercivity, and a thin hysteresis loop"},"phy-12-11-m21":{"idea":"A permanent magnet needs to hold its magnetism strongly.","steps":["High retentivity keeps a strong field.","High coercivity makes it hard to demagnetise.","Steel and alnico have both."],"others":"Low retentivity or low coercivity would lose magnetism easily. Copper and bismuth are not ferromagnetic.","trap":"Permanent magnet: high retentivity and high coercivity.","answer":"a high retentivity and a high coercivity, such as steel or alnico"},"phy-12-11-m22":{"idea":"Soft iron shields the space inside it because field lines prefer to go through the iron.","steps":["Soft iron has a very high permeability.","Field lines crowd into the walls of the shell.","Very few lines go through the cavity, so the field inside is very small."],"others":"The field is not the same as outside, nor stronger inside. It is not necessarily the Earth's field.","trap":"This is magnetic shielding.","answer":"very small, as the field lines go through the walls of the iron"},"phy-12-11-n01":{"idea":"Torque on a dipole is mB sin θ.","steps":["m = 0.50 A m², B = 0.20 T, θ = 30°.","sin 30° = 0.5.","τ = 0.50 × 0.20 × 0.5 = 0.050 N m."],"trap":"θ is the angle between m and B.","answer":"0.050 N m"},"phy-12-11-n02":{"idea":"Potential energy of a dipole: U = −mB cos θ, with U = 0 at 90°.","steps":["m = 0.50, B = 0.20, θ = 60°, cos 60° = 0.5.","U = −0.50 × 0.20 × 0.5.","U = −0.050 J."],"trap":"The sign is negative: the dipole is nearer the stable position than at 90°.","answer":"-0.050 J"},"phy-12-11-n03":{"idea":"A small magnet oscillates with T = 2π√(I/(mB)).","steps":["I/(mB) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁶/(2.0 × 3.0 × 10⁻⁵) = 0.025.","√0.025 = 0.1581.","T = 2π × 0.1581 = 0.993 s."],"trap":"Check the units: kg m²/(A m² T) works out to s².","answer":"0.993 s"},"phy-12-11-n04":{"idea":"Magnetisation is M = χ H.","steps":["χ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ and H = 1.0 × 10⁴ A/m.","M = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.0 × 10⁴.","M = 0.20 A/m."],"trap":"Check: B = μ₀(H + M) gives the same M.","answer":"0.20 A/m"},"phy-12-11-n05":{"idea":"Curie's law: χ T is constant.","steps":["χ₂ = χ₁ T₁/T₂.","χ₂ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 300/400.","χ₂ = 1.50 × 10⁻⁴."],"trap":"A hotter sample has a smaller χ.","answer":"1.50 (in units of 10⁻⁴)"},"phy-12-11-n06":{"idea":"Bohr magneton: μ_B = eh/(4πmₑ).","steps":["e h = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 1.0608 × 10⁻⁵².","4π mₑ = 4π × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ = 1.1448 × 10⁻²⁹.","μ_B = 1.0608 × 10⁻⁵²/1.1448 × 10⁻²⁹ = 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ A m²."],"trap":"Use 4π, not 2π.","answer":"9.27 (in units of 10⁻²⁴ A m²)"},"phy-12-11-n07":{"idea":"A core multiplies the field by its relative permeability.","steps":["B = μ₀ μᵣ n I.","μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷, μᵣ = 500, n = 1000, I = 0.50 A.","B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 500 × 1000 × 0.50 = 0.314 T."],"trap":"Without the core, B would be 500 times smaller: 6.28 × 10⁻⁴ T.","answer":"0.314 T"}};
